1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,360
From paper clips and typewriters to palm pilots and laptop computers, it's a jumble out there.

2
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:15,440
Your office is home to miracles of efficient communication that are a staple of a productive workplace.

3
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:20,140
Office Wonders, next on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
This office is a model of efficiency.

5
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,700
Built just a few days ago, but older than history.

6
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:51,160
Workers visit cubicles.

7
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:55,220
Quotas are met, productivity is high.

8
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:58,700
The place is literally buzzing.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,220
Look closer.

10
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:05,160
Worker bees.

11
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:07,000
Drones.

12
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:09,020
Desk jockeys.

13
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:10,800
Pencil pushers.

14
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:12,600
Paper shufflers.

15
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:14,940
Computer nerds.

16
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:16,520
Office geeks.

17
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:18,960
They punch keys.

18
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:20,580
Juggle schedules.

19
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:22,560
Slash budgets.

20
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:24,380
Hack computers.

21
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:26,740
Wrestle paper tigers.

22
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,900
Who says a day at the office isn't excited?

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,760
It's a hive of activity, the office.

24
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:40,000
Over 130 million of America's busy bees spend an average of over 39 hours a week there.

25
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:45,260
They consume nearly 100 million tons of paper a year

26
00:01:45,261 --> 00:01:48,900
and spend billions of dollars on equipment annually.

27
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:52,240
Look even closer.

28
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:59,520
Ever notice that this seemingly humble office is ground zero for the technology explosion?

29
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,480
It has been for the past 150 years.

30
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:11,380
Computers, telephones, fax machines, copiers, even scotch tape.

31
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:18,820
It all begins with a marvel so simple, so basic, so thin, that it's easy to overlook.

32
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:21,760
Without it, there would be no office.

33
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:24,900
The whole thing is supported by a sheet of paper.

34
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:27,440
Paper was invented in China.

35
00:02:27,441 --> 00:02:41,441
In 200 B.C., it went to Korea in 600, Japan 700, and then it went across the top of Africa, and then Europe, and it arrived in 1259, in the United States in 1690.

36
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:46,460
Paper was a big deal because of what it was not.

37
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:50,500
Not cumbersome, nor heavy, nor hard to store, nor short-lived.

38
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:54,200
Before paper, people wrote on all sorts of things.

39
00:02:54,201 --> 00:03:00,860
They would write on cuneiform nails, which were clay nails that they would put in the sides of buildings.

40
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:03,960
In Thailand, they would use palm leaves.

41
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,580
The most popular prepaper was pounded bark.

42
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:13,060
It's hard to shove stone tablets into a file cabinet or alphabetize palm leaves,

43
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:16,680
but reams of paper can be stored in very small areas.

44
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:19,560
Paper can be collated, sorted, filed.

45
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:21,580
Plus, paper is easy to produce.

46
00:03:21,581 --> 00:03:28,580
Start by beating down some wood fibers or cotton blue jeans into a pulp in a big vat.

47
00:03:28,581 --> 00:03:32,580
What we have right here is our mold and our deckle.

48
00:03:32,581 --> 00:03:37,580
We take our mold and deckle together like this and you bring it down into your vat.

49
00:03:37,581 --> 00:03:40,580
You shake slightly as it's coming up.

50
00:03:40,581 --> 00:03:43,580
And the water will filter through.

51
00:03:43,581 --> 00:03:46,580
Remove the deckle.

52
00:03:46,581 --> 00:03:53,580
The deckle is a type of frame which shapes the paper material onto the mold.

53
00:03:53,581 --> 00:03:56,580
Place a blotter down.

54
00:03:56,581 --> 00:03:59,580
Put that right on there.

55
00:03:59,581 --> 00:04:05,580
What we are doing is adhering the paper to the blotter.

56
00:04:05,581 --> 00:04:08,580
And we'll continue this process.

57
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:11,580
We'll keep putting blotters down.

58
00:04:11,581 --> 00:04:18,020
It's quicker when it's done at a paper mill, which uses wood pulp to produce massive reams of paper for all sorts of uses,

59
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:24,480
including product labels, fine writing tablets, even those hospital nightgowns with the embarrassing flaps.

60
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:29,780
The earliest paper makers saw an advantage in diversifying.

61
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:35,380
The first colored paper was in China in about 600 A.D.

62
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:40,980
It was a yellow paper, and it was actually covered with a poisonous solution.

63
00:04:40,981 --> 00:04:43,740
for an insecticide.

64
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:48,520
Envelopes began in Korea in 600 A.D.

65
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:52,800
Monks used lined paper in the Middle Ages to keep their calligraphy straight.

66
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:57,040
People made paper out of fishing nets,

67
00:04:58,100 --> 00:04:58,940
blue jeans,

68
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:00,600
cotton,

69
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:01,960
straw,

70
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:03,440
corn husks,

71
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:06,160
practically anything that could be pounded flat.

72
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:10,200
And with paper comes a host of other office wonders

73
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:12,180
that are as simple as they are essential.

74
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:16,340
Pencils can trace their lineage to the Roman stylus,

75
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:18,600
a thin rod often made of lead.

76
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:23,160
Graphite, the softest of solid minerals

77
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:25,500
and the purest form of carbon after diamonds,

78
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:29,020
ultimately replaced lead because it made a darker mark

79
00:05:29,021 --> 00:05:30,800
and was non-toxic.

80
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:33,080
But it was too soft,

81
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:35,360
so people hollowed out strips of wood

82
00:05:35,361 --> 00:05:37,160
and shoved in graphite sticks.

83
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:39,220
By the early 19th century,

84
00:05:39,221 --> 00:05:42,960
companies began manufacturing wooden pencils on a large scale.

85
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:47,080
Most pencils today are made of incense cedar.

86
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:50,600
The number two is a measure of the hardness of the writing core.

87
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:54,100
The familiar Ticonderoga pencil was painted yellow

88
00:05:54,101 --> 00:05:57,240
to indicate it used superior graphite from China.

89
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:01,820
The pencil is an office mainstay,

90
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:04,840
with two billion sold worldwide each year,

91
00:06:04,841 --> 00:06:08,320
all capable of writing 45,000 words.

92
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:18,400
In addition to pencils, pens, often made from the flight feathers of geese, dipped in ink, helped people get their ideas across.

93
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:26,840
Ink could be made from berries, water, oil, soot, squid, though it too was being mass-produced by the 19th century.

94
00:06:26,841 --> 00:06:39,220
Markers and highlighters also contribute to the over 8 billion writing instruments purchased worldwide each year at a cost of over $2 billion.

95
00:06:39,221 --> 00:06:39,921
dollars.

96
00:06:39,922 --> 00:06:46,340
In 1963, a group of our lab people in Cambridge, Massachusetts at the Carters

97
00:06:46,341 --> 00:06:49,460
Ink Company had been working on children's products.

98
00:06:49,461 --> 00:06:51,500
When they took a children's marker

99
00:06:51,501 --> 00:06:56,440
and wrote over text, they could see the text through the line that they had written, and

100
00:06:56,441 --> 00:07:00,253
thus was born the highlighter. The first highlighters were yellow.

101
00:07:00,254 --> 00:07:02,160
Today, you can get them in nearly

102
00:07:02,161 --> 00:07:05,899
any color. Highlighters do more than just emphasize copy.

103
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:08,720
We find a lot of different uses that people

104
00:07:08,721 --> 00:07:11,647
have used the products for over the years.

105
00:07:11,648 --> 00:07:15,340
Anything from doing artwork to decorating their skin.

106
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:20,200
Unfortunately, sometimes people also use permanent marker on their skin, and we have to help them

107
00:07:20,201 --> 00:07:23,514
in correcting those bad product decisions.

108
00:07:23,515 --> 00:07:27,380
With all these papers flying around, devices were

109
00:07:27,381 --> 00:07:28,855
needed to sort them out.

110
00:07:28,856 --> 00:07:33,220
In the 1890s, people started bending pieces of wire and patenting them.

111
00:07:33,221 --> 00:07:39,120
The gem-style paperclip, the most successful but not the only paper fastener, was born.

112
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:46,100
In 1914, the Boston Wire Stitcher Company introduced a desk-sized stapler

113
00:07:46,101 --> 00:07:50,900
that sent a small, sharp wire into a stack of papers, or the occasional finger.

114
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:57,420
In 1903, the Clipper Manufacturing Company began producing metal clasps.

115
00:07:57,421 --> 00:08:01,600
There were other ways to collect paper

116
00:08:01,601 --> 00:08:04,400
Notebooks, index cards, file folders

117
00:08:04,401 --> 00:08:06,420
Not to mention three ring binders

118
00:08:06,421 --> 00:08:11,460
Up until probably just in recent history

119
00:08:11,461 --> 00:08:14,820
Most binders were very plain, very boring

120
00:08:14,821 --> 00:08:16,960
Had no style to them at all

121
00:08:16,961 --> 00:08:20,300
The traditional market of office consumption

122
00:08:20,301 --> 00:08:22,580
Is pretty boring actually

123
00:08:22,581 --> 00:08:25,640
The best seller today continues to be black

124
00:08:25,641 --> 00:08:32,940
As offices got busier, mishaps were bound to occur.

125
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:38,240
This engineer named Richard Drew, working for the 3M company in the 1920s,

126
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:43,440
made everyone's life easier when he got his hands on some new material called cellophane

127
00:08:43,441 --> 00:08:45,900
and invented Scotch tape.

128
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:49,940
When Richard Drew saw this material, he coated some adhesive on it,

129
00:08:49,941 --> 00:08:57,320
slid it into tape and sent it to a company in Chicago that wanted to use it to wrap bakery goods.

130
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:00,400
And in 1930, Scotch cellophane tape was born.

131
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:05,340
3M followed Scotch tape with a tape dispenser in 1932.

132
00:09:06,280 --> 00:09:10,200
The response to Scotch cellophane tape was overwhelming for 3M.

133
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:14,980
Introducing a product during the first year of the Depression would not be considered very good timing.

134
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:19,120
3M was one of the few companies not to lay off people during the Depression.

135
00:09:19,940 --> 00:09:28,800
It didn't take long for Scotch tape to wend its way into offices, fixing torn pages and sealing envelopes.

136
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:33,220
Today, the company produces over 900 different Scotch products.

137
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:39,020
Over 90% of the offices and households in America have a roll of Scotch tape.

138
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:44,580
Paper, pencils, and tape are crucial, though low-tech wonders.

139
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:47,120
But at the height of the Industrial Revolution,

140
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:51,140
two high-tech inventions would usher in the age of the modern office.

141
00:09:51,980 --> 00:09:54,600
Next, the telephone and the typewriter.

142
00:09:57,620 --> 00:10:01,980
Dallas Secretary Betty Nesmith, who invented liquid paper in 1956,

143
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:05,120
is the mother of Michael Nesmith, of the Monkees.

144
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:08,980
Office Wonders will return on Modern Marvels.

145
00:10:08,981 --> 00:10:15,260
During the 19th century, people began to realize the value of time.

146
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:19,220
Before this, things happened at a slower pace because they had to.

147
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:22,180
We traveled at the speed of horse.

148
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:25,460
Distant communication was slow and trade slower.

149
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:28,280
Most offices conducted business with their neighbors.

150
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:30,860
Time crept along unhurried.

151
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:33,840
Then came the railroads.

152
00:10:33,841 --> 00:10:37,240
People could ship products further, faster, and to more customers.

153
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:42,400
The better you made use of time, the more products you sold, and the more money you earned.

154
00:10:43,660 --> 00:10:45,540
Efficiency became the name of the game.

155
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:47,680
Along came the telegraph.

156
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:50,680
And people conducted business by electricity.

157
00:10:51,420 --> 00:10:54,060
Wiring orders for goods in a matter of hours.

158
00:10:55,640 --> 00:10:59,280
The more you sold, the more paperwork you needed to process.

159
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:01,920
Enter the typewriter.

160
00:11:01,921 --> 00:11:09,240
The typewriter was invented at a time when the American factory system was really building steam.

161
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:15,060
There were consumer goods being churned out of America's factories at a tremendous rate.

162
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:19,020
But in the front office, there was a huge backlog

163
00:11:19,021 --> 00:11:24,560
because all the paperwork that went along with the products was done by hand.

164
00:11:25,260 --> 00:11:29,160
So American industry needed the typewriter to do the paperwork

165
00:11:29,161 --> 00:11:32,720
to keep up with what the factories were churning out.

166
00:11:34,540 --> 00:11:37,460
Christopher Latham Scholes, an inventor from Milwaukee,

167
00:11:37,660 --> 00:11:39,740
produced the Scholes and Glidden typewriter,

168
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:42,140
which went on the market in 1874.

169
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:45,160
The concept was simple.

170
00:11:45,680 --> 00:11:47,260
You press a button linked to a lever,

171
00:11:47,261 --> 00:11:50,560
which causes an arm to swing up and whack against an inked ribbon

172
00:11:50,561 --> 00:11:51,920
in front of a piece of paper.

173
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:54,920
This was the first typewriter.

174
00:11:55,220 --> 00:11:59,080
It is decorated with Victorian floral decals,

175
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:01,383
gold curly cues, but there's no mistake.

176
00:12:01,384 --> 00:12:04,440
It is a typewriter and it is the machine from which all

177
00:12:04,441 --> 00:12:06,204
modern typewriters evolved.

178
00:12:06,205 --> 00:12:10,320
Within five years, most of the bugs had been worked out and out

179
00:12:10,321 --> 00:12:16,760
came a second model. The Remington No. 2 was really a perfected machine.

180
00:12:16,761 --> 00:12:18,280
It was eventually

181
00:12:18,281 --> 00:12:24,504
very successful and it really became the archetype of old typewriters.

182
00:12:24,505 --> 00:12:26,460
Big black box of keys.

183
00:12:26,461 --> 00:12:33,520
Typewriters epitomized the spirit of invention that swept late 19th century industrial America.

184
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:39,740
The real challenge in building a typewriter was achieving precision.

185
00:12:40,380 --> 00:12:46,660
Because you had hundreds of parts that all had to work together to print a character on the page,

186
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:52,960
space it one character down for the next one, type uniform straight lines.

187
00:12:52,961 --> 00:12:57,960
Typewriters helped people standardize and streamline.

188
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:04,840
For instance, the 8.5 by 11-inch sheet of paper used for business correspondence was established by the typewriter.

189
00:13:05,740 --> 00:13:10,440
And carbon copy invoices were more legible when they were typed rather than handwritten.

190
00:13:14,460 --> 00:13:18,720
Soon, typewriters began streamlining, too, and adding improvements.

191
00:13:18,721 --> 00:13:22,560
The shift key provided access both to upper and lowercase letters.

192
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:26,960
thus twice as many characters.

193
00:13:26,961 --> 00:13:30,960
The keyboard was laid out in what's called a QWERTY keyboard,

194
00:13:30,961 --> 00:13:37,960
keeping common pairs of letters apart and preventing the typewriter arms from rubbing and sticking together.

195
00:13:37,961 --> 00:13:40,960
The tab key arrived in the 1890s,

196
00:13:40,961 --> 00:13:43,960
allowing columns for numbers and counting.

197
00:13:43,961 --> 00:13:46,960
In 1895, the Underwood came out.

198
00:13:46,961 --> 00:13:51,960
That was a typewriter in which the type bars struck the platen from the front.

199
00:13:51,961 --> 00:13:54,220
you could actually see what you were doing.

200
00:13:54,940 --> 00:13:57,800
Leanderwood was the first modern typewriter.

201
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:02,740
Some features were there from the start.

202
00:14:03,300 --> 00:14:04,620
Setting margins, for instance,

203
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:08,680
and the ubiquitous little bell that warns you when you're about to type off the paper.

204
00:14:09,340 --> 00:14:10,820
It's now a cultural fixture.

205
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:14,920
Innovations would follow.

206
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:18,080
A portable model showed up in 1884.

207
00:14:18,081 --> 00:14:22,840
An electric one in 1902.

208
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:27,400
Electricity being scarce in 1902, it didn't really wow the business world.

209
00:14:27,940 --> 00:14:31,740
It was left to a time clock and punch card sorting company called IBM

210
00:14:31,741 --> 00:14:35,260
to make the first successful electric model in 1933.

211
00:14:36,380 --> 00:14:40,600
In 1961, they wowed business again with their Selectric typewriter.

212
00:14:41,160 --> 00:14:44,800
The IBM Selectric was a groundbreaking typewriter.

213
00:14:44,801 --> 00:14:48,260
You had a machine in which the carriage did not move.

214
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:50,540
The element moved side to side.

215
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:53,480
And this little ball danced along the paper.

216
00:14:53,620 --> 00:14:55,080
It was wonderful to watch.

217
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:56,420
It worked beautifully.

218
00:14:57,380 --> 00:15:01,500
Typewriter manufacturers introduced another office innovation, the female.

219
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:04,900
The first makers of the typewriter, the Remington Company,

220
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:08,960
actively recruited women to learn how to use the machine

221
00:15:08,961 --> 00:15:12,120
so that they could be hired as operators of the machine.

222
00:15:12,121 --> 00:15:18,440
This brought women into the office world, which previously had been an all-male environment.

223
00:15:19,320 --> 00:15:23,420
One of the advantages of women workers was that they were cheaper to hire than men.

224
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:30,580
A lot of women were deliberately paid less because the attitude was, well, they're living at home.

225
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:33,920
They're not supporting a family, therefore they don't need the money.

226
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:36,020
They have a father to help them out.

227
00:15:36,021 --> 00:15:43,960
And even if the woman was a single independent woman, they were still scrambling to get by.

228
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:51,820
Women would be impacted by another great 19th century office wonder, Alexander Graham Bell's telephone.

229
00:15:52,720 --> 00:16:01,240
When you speak into the telephone, the transmitter takes your sound waves and converts them into electrical waves.

230
00:16:01,241 --> 00:16:08,540
You will into an analog, a waveform that has the same shape as the sound waves, but as an electrical wave.

231
00:16:09,240 --> 00:16:17,160
This electrical wave can then travel down a wire in the simplest of telephones to another telephone,

232
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:26,520
where the receiver will take that signal and convert it back into a sound wave, which you can hold up to your ear and hear.

233
00:16:26,521 --> 00:16:37,400
Phones were introduced in 1876, marketed in 1877, and by 1878 there was a need for the first central switchboard run by operators.

234
00:16:38,660 --> 00:16:45,220
Women would largely make up the workforce of telephone operators as the revolutionary device took offices by storm.

235
00:16:45,221 --> 00:16:58,460
The men who actually ran the telephone industry in the early days saw the telephone preeminently as a business tool.

236
00:16:59,340 --> 00:17:03,480
From the first, there were both business and residential subscribers.

237
00:17:04,120 --> 00:17:10,440
But even in the marketing to residential customers, the emphasis was on the business use.

238
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:13,960
And businesses bought telephones like hotcakes.

239
00:17:13,961 --> 00:17:22,800
The first telephone directory in New York City, and it's primarily business customers, fit on a single sheet of paper.

240
00:17:23,320 --> 00:17:29,300
By the directory for 1900, you're dealing with a fat book of almost 600 pages.

241
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:38,960
By the 1890s, desk models appeared, which were standardized into the familiar candlestick phone by the turn of the century.

242
00:17:38,961 --> 00:17:44,440
multiple line phones appeared in 1892

243
00:17:44,441 --> 00:17:50,760
sub switchboards called private branch exchanges or pbx's appeared in the 1890s

244
00:17:50,761 --> 00:17:54,960
office buildings could now have a local network

245
00:17:54,961 --> 00:18:01,640
at&t itself american telephone and telegraph company name first appears in 1885

246
00:18:01,641 --> 00:18:09,540
It was incorporated specifically to build and operate the first long-distance network in the United States.

247
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:18,280
It started construction in New York, building out in every direction, and reached its initial goal, Chicago, in 1892.

248
00:18:20,740 --> 00:18:24,900
Long-distance calls were nearly all business-related due to the cost.

249
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:30,120
$9 allowed New York to speak with Chicago for a minute in 1892,

250
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:34,560
which is the equivalent of about $100 in today's money.

251
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:41,840
Similarly, when long-distance service became available from the Atlantic to the Pacific,

252
00:18:42,380 --> 00:18:45,220
the initial rate was $20.70.

253
00:18:45,960 --> 00:18:51,040
With such prices, calls tended to be made for those uses

254
00:18:51,041 --> 00:18:55,500
where the financial benefits were commensurate with the cost.

255
00:18:56,080 --> 00:18:59,240
But for business, instantaneous communication was worth it.

256
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:07,120
The telephone made it possible to more closely coordinate economic activities,

257
00:19:07,300 --> 00:19:13,240
business activities occurring in different places than any previous device.

258
00:19:14,600 --> 00:19:19,340
There are some advertising brochures that AT&T put out in the 1890s.

259
00:19:19,341 --> 00:19:28,840
On them it says, the mail is quick, the telegraph is quicker, but the telephone is instantaneous and you don't have to wait for an answer.

260
00:19:29,740 --> 00:19:30,540
It's interactive.

261
00:19:31,680 --> 00:19:34,780
And businesses seized on this.

262
00:19:36,300 --> 00:19:41,460
In 1927, North America and Europe could talk for $25 a minute.

263
00:19:41,461 --> 00:19:49,940
Other crucial business innovations included the hold button and key sets with extra rows of buttons for tailored office tasks.

264
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:54,860
Mobile phone service began in St. Louis in 1946.

265
00:19:56,180 --> 00:20:01,320
Speaker phones arrived in the 1950s and touch tone service in 1963.

266
00:20:02,120 --> 00:20:04,060
Toll free 800 numbers soon followed.

267
00:20:04,580 --> 00:20:08,860
AT&T introduced the first answering machines in 1953.

268
00:20:08,861 --> 00:20:12,500
marketed just towards business customers.

269
00:20:13,380 --> 00:20:16,760
Another innovation that's older than people think

270
00:20:16,761 --> 00:20:20,180
and was primarily used by businesses in the early days

271
00:20:20,181 --> 00:20:22,660
were telephone credit cards.

272
00:20:23,200 --> 00:20:26,680
AT&T introduced the telephone credit card in 1939.

273
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:30,720
And it was designed for people like traveling salesmen

274
00:20:30,721 --> 00:20:33,780
who'd have frequent needs to call home from the road.

275
00:20:34,740 --> 00:20:38,300
The telephone allows businesses to run at the speed of sound.

276
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:42,040
Today, it is unimaginable to think of an office without a telephone,

277
00:20:42,600 --> 00:20:46,740
just as it is unimaginable to think of an office without a copying machine.

278
00:20:47,340 --> 00:20:50,920
But it took clever marketing on the part of a little company called Xerox

279
00:20:50,921 --> 00:20:55,900
to convince businesses that they would ever need to make hundreds of copies of the same sheet of paper.

280
00:20:56,580 --> 00:20:59,620
Next, all about facsimiles and copies.

281
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:08,640
Alexander Graham Bell's cousin, Chichester Bell, was an early patent holder in 1886.

282
00:21:08,860 --> 00:21:12,000
of an office dictation machine that would become the dictaphone.

283
00:21:12,960 --> 00:21:15,860
Office Wonders will return on Modern Marvels.

284
00:21:16,880 --> 00:21:21,320
By the 1920s and 30s, offices were becoming models of efficiency.

285
00:21:22,080 --> 00:21:23,180
Uniformity was key.

286
00:21:23,760 --> 00:21:27,100
The parts of the office were like interchangeable cogs in a great machine.

287
00:21:27,540 --> 00:21:29,720
If a cog or a worker broke,

288
00:21:30,140 --> 00:21:32,700
another identical one could be put in the same place.

289
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:35,860
If you think about the design of offices,

290
00:21:35,861 --> 00:21:41,880
There is a little parallel here to the design of early factories.

291
00:21:42,020 --> 00:21:47,440
When the factories first started appearing, they were designed to get the maximum use of the machine.

292
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:50,460
So forget the human operatives, who cared about them.

293
00:21:50,580 --> 00:21:51,980
There were plenty more where they came from.

294
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:59,160
Huge office buildings had rows and rows of identical desks and lamps and workers behind them.

295
00:21:59,780 --> 00:22:02,100
Every once in a while, someone would jump out the window.

296
00:22:02,660 --> 00:22:04,160
But other than that, it seemed to work.

297
00:22:04,840 --> 00:22:08,340
What the office lacked in personality, it made up for in efficiency.

298
00:22:09,980 --> 00:22:16,980
It seems natural for office managers to take advantage of a machine that could duplicate documents exactly, but they didn't.

299
00:22:17,640 --> 00:22:20,160
First of all, carbon copies existed.

300
00:22:21,280 --> 00:22:27,060
But carbon copies could only make three or four copies at best, and they were hard to deal with.

301
00:22:29,140 --> 00:22:33,160
But a little company named Haloid was about to change everything.

302
00:22:33,161 --> 00:22:38,180
The Haloid Company started here in Rochester, a very, very small company,

303
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:41,900
actually in competition with Eastman Kodak.

304
00:22:42,220 --> 00:22:44,420
And they started to search for new products.

305
00:22:45,580 --> 00:22:50,000
And they were looking for something new and different for the future.

306
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:53,260
They found it with a lawyer named Chester Carlson.

307
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,280
He actually was educated as a physicist.

308
00:22:56,281 --> 00:23:09,520
Now, back in the early 30s, he, as a patent attorney, had to make copies, and he was very dissatisfied with what was available.

309
00:23:09,820 --> 00:23:14,880
So he actually started out saying, I'm not going to use chemicals.

310
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:21,020
I want to use some physical principles because I'm aware of physics.

311
00:23:21,021 --> 00:23:26,320
and he started the early work on what we call electrostatic printing.

312
00:23:28,020 --> 00:23:30,320
Electrostatic printing would become xerography,

313
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:32,840
from the Greek words for dry and writing.

314
00:23:34,680 --> 00:23:39,000
Basically, a light passes over an image placed on photoconductive material,

315
00:23:39,460 --> 00:23:42,480
increasing the electrical conductivity of that material.

316
00:23:43,240 --> 00:23:45,500
The pattern can then be repeated on another surface

317
00:23:45,501 --> 00:23:48,000
when a powder is passed over that surface,

318
00:23:48,380 --> 00:23:49,680
clinging to the charged portions.

319
00:23:51,020 --> 00:23:58,260
The first demonstration of this phenomenon took place on October 22, 1938, in Astoria, Queens, New York.

320
00:23:58,261 --> 00:24:01,940
But Carlson had trouble convincing others of Xerography's potential.

321
00:24:03,160 --> 00:24:06,660
He was a lousy salesman, and he went out in the countryside,

322
00:24:07,780 --> 00:24:16,640
and actually visited 27 companies, including IBM, Eastman Kodak, Bell & Howell.

323
00:24:16,641 --> 00:24:24,100
He went to everybody trying to sell him on the idea of xerography, and nobody was interested.

324
00:24:25,320 --> 00:24:32,440
Nobody, that is, except for Haloy, which spent the next few years turning Carlson's ideas into a working machine.

325
00:24:33,400 --> 00:24:38,320
First true copy that made copy easy was the Xerox 914.

326
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:42,220
It was announced in October of 1959.

327
00:24:42,221 --> 00:24:45,900
The first machine was delivered in March 1960.

328
00:24:46,640 --> 00:24:53,480
They were able to put an original in the platinum glass, dial in the number of copies they want,

329
00:24:54,040 --> 00:24:56,540
press the button, and they were finished.

330
00:24:57,120 --> 00:24:58,460
The copies came out.

331
00:25:00,340 --> 00:25:02,920
Hayloid built it, but did anyone really want it?

332
00:25:03,420 --> 00:25:07,560
The company was running out of money and started searching for a partner with deep pockets.

333
00:25:08,100 --> 00:25:09,380
They approached IBM.

334
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:12,640
Now, IBM went to Arthur B. Little.

335
00:25:13,140 --> 00:25:15,540
Very, very fine market research outfit.

336
00:25:15,541 --> 00:25:19,680
and said to Arthur D. Little, do you think there's a market for this machine?

337
00:25:21,180 --> 00:25:23,780
And Arthur D. Little went out and did a market research,

338
00:25:24,040 --> 00:25:32,160
and they came back and said, 5,000 units will saturate the market.

339
00:25:32,740 --> 00:25:34,600
There was a big meeting at the Haloid Company,

340
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:38,460
and we decided to go ahead on our own.

341
00:25:38,880 --> 00:25:44,620
Now, we went ahead spending money we didn't have for a product

342
00:25:44,621 --> 00:25:46,440
that nobody wanted.

343
00:25:47,140 --> 00:25:49,700
If Harvard Business School studied this thing,

344
00:25:50,220 --> 00:25:53,480
they'd say that was a course for disaster,

345
00:25:54,060 --> 00:25:56,800
that you guys were going down the wrong alleyway,

346
00:25:57,160 --> 00:25:58,380
and it was pretty stupid.

347
00:25:59,420 --> 00:26:01,200
But Hayloid had a plan.

348
00:26:01,700 --> 00:26:04,920
They would rent out the 914 for $95 a month,

349
00:26:05,180 --> 00:26:08,200
even though each machine cost over $2,000 to manufacture.

350
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:13,480
Each customer would get 2,000 free copies each month,

351
00:26:13,481 --> 00:26:16,600
with subsequent copies charged at five cents apiece.

352
00:26:17,680 --> 00:26:21,400
Much to everyone's surprise, businesses went copy crazy.

353
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:27,480
They copied memos, invoices, articles, recipes, cartoons.

354
00:26:28,700 --> 00:26:32,020
Over the next few years, Haloid focused on xerography.

355
00:26:32,740 --> 00:26:37,920
And rechristened Xerox produced over 200,000 914s.

356
00:26:37,921 --> 00:26:40,660
we calculate

357
00:26:40,661 --> 00:26:43,720
that over 600,000

358
00:26:43,721 --> 00:26:44,540
placements

359
00:26:44,541 --> 00:26:46,780
were made of the 914

360
00:26:46,781 --> 00:26:48,260
during its lifetime

361
00:26:48,261 --> 00:26:50,720
this is the machine that Arthur D. Little

362
00:26:50,721 --> 00:26:52,960
said 5,000

363
00:26:52,961 --> 00:26:54,780
would saturate the market

364
00:26:54,781 --> 00:26:58,100
how successful was it?

365
00:26:58,540 --> 00:26:59,920
according to Fortune magazine

366
00:26:59,921 --> 00:27:03,000
probably the hottest return on investment

367
00:27:03,001 --> 00:27:04,560
of any commercial product

368
00:27:04,561 --> 00:27:12,560
Today, copiers are faster, more reliable, and studded with features, including collating,

369
00:27:12,561 --> 00:27:17,560
stapling, enlarging and reducing, image rotation, and color.

370
00:27:17,561 --> 00:27:22,560
The product that nobody wanted is now indispensable.

371
00:27:22,561 --> 00:27:27,560
Though other companies now make copiers, the name Xerox is synonymous with copying.

372
00:27:27,561 --> 00:27:32,560
Copiers change the office environment in really one fundamental way, and that's communication.

373
00:27:32,561 --> 00:27:36,740
Prior to the office copier, communication was very difficult in the office.

374
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:44,480
And that really was the change that the copier brought to corporate America and then on a worldwide basis.

375
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:49,400
Today it improves workflow. It improves productivity of everyone.

376
00:27:51,100 --> 00:27:57,880
Now, if only you could take a page and send a copy of it somehow, instantaneously, to someone far away.

377
00:27:58,480 --> 00:28:00,080
That would be impressive.

378
00:28:00,081 --> 00:28:10,080
Scotsman Alexander Bain thought so in 1842 when he invented the first facsimile machine using old clock parts and electrically conductive paper.

379
00:28:10,081 --> 00:28:19,080
It was a workable machine, but since it predated the telegraph and the telephone and their electrical high wires, Bain's machine drifted into oblivion.

380
00:28:19,081 --> 00:28:22,520
years later and quite independently

381
00:28:22,521 --> 00:28:26,320
Elijah Gray in this country tried his hand at the fax machine

382
00:28:26,321 --> 00:28:30,740
creating the tele-autograph which used a stylus connected to a metal plate

383
00:28:30,741 --> 00:28:35,180
much like the telephone in the telegraph

384
00:28:35,181 --> 00:28:38,960
a signal generated by the stylus is translated into an analog

385
00:28:38,961 --> 00:28:42,600
and sent along wires electrically

386
00:28:42,601 --> 00:28:46,640
then reconstructed at the other end into a facsimile of the original image

387
00:28:46,641 --> 00:28:53,320
These early machines began turning up in banks, hotels, and even racetracks.

388
00:28:54,180 --> 00:28:58,100
Eventually, Elisha Gray's tell autograph company became OmniFax,

389
00:28:58,420 --> 00:29:02,200
which produced more modern facsimile machines in the 1960s.

390
00:29:04,540 --> 00:29:08,520
By the 70s and 80s, the familiar facts became a phenomenon

391
00:29:08,521 --> 00:29:12,660
as the machines began chirping like birds to each other all over the world.

392
00:29:13,480 --> 00:29:16,540
There's a handshake that takes place before the transmission actually happens.

393
00:29:16,640 --> 00:29:19,400
to establish compatibility between the two units.

394
00:29:20,140 --> 00:29:23,340
In the late 1990s, Xerox bought OmniFax

395
00:29:23,341 --> 00:29:26,600
and began rethinking the fax machine's purpose as office equipment.

396
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:31,560
When you think about it, a fax machine really consists of a printer,

397
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:35,540
just like any other printer, consists of a scanner, just like any other scanner,

398
00:29:35,960 --> 00:29:39,740
and then some control electronics and communications electronics

399
00:29:39,741 --> 00:29:43,400
to tie the pieces together and to make them work over a telephone line.

400
00:29:43,401 --> 00:29:47,260
And when you put a scanner and printer together, you have a copy machine as well.

401
00:29:47,580 --> 00:29:50,540
So you can put all those pieces together in a very effective machine.

402
00:29:52,380 --> 00:29:56,660
Carlson, Bain, and Gray probably didn't envision that as they toiled away in their labs.

403
00:29:57,080 --> 00:30:05,080
Nor did they likely envision a device that threatened to make all their revolutionary ideas, even paper itself, obsolete.

404
00:30:05,920 --> 00:30:08,140
Next, the personal computer.

405
00:30:08,141 --> 00:30:15,080
Consumers spend nearly $900 million annually on ballpoint pens,

406
00:30:15,081 --> 00:30:19,240
though most individuals borrow theirs from the office supply cabinet.

407
00:30:19,900 --> 00:30:21,360
Modern Marvels will return.

408
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:27,820
The modern computer began so innocently as a way to quickly count heads.

409
00:30:28,660 --> 00:30:30,220
All the heads in the whole country.

410
00:30:31,300 --> 00:30:35,360
Census takers took forever to analyze the information they got from their statistics.

411
00:30:35,361 --> 00:30:40,820
By the time they figured it all out, it was time to start the count all over again.

412
00:30:41,420 --> 00:30:42,680
There had to be a better way.

413
00:30:43,720 --> 00:30:46,320
But there wasn't. Not yet, anyway.

414
00:30:47,880 --> 00:30:52,440
Census takers needed some sort of tabulating machine that would speed the process up.

415
00:30:53,420 --> 00:30:58,240
And to do that, they would need some form of data or some way of collecting the information.

416
00:30:58,620 --> 00:31:03,540
So they created some cards that could have holes punched in them.

417
00:31:03,541 --> 00:31:10,240
And they created a tabulating machine, which would take these cards and tabulate the results.

418
00:31:11,440 --> 00:31:17,320
If tabulating machines or calculators could count heads, maybe they could figure other things.

419
00:31:18,340 --> 00:31:23,420
The first real machine built was by the U.S. Army to do ballistics calculations,

420
00:31:23,660 --> 00:31:29,480
to try and assist in improving artillery tables based upon wind and weather and things like that.

421
00:31:29,481 --> 00:31:34,760
So it was a way to alleviate and speed up computation when you need it.

422
00:31:35,420 --> 00:31:38,360
So the initial intent was numeric applications.

423
00:31:40,900 --> 00:31:43,100
These were no pocket calculators.

424
00:31:44,480 --> 00:31:51,000
Some of them, like the ENIAC, for example, was 100 feet long, 10 feet wide, several feet high.

425
00:31:52,300 --> 00:31:55,300
The early machines had 18,000 vacuum tubes in them.

426
00:31:55,301 --> 00:32:00,720
They weighed tons and used 200,000 watts of power.

427
00:32:01,380 --> 00:32:08,760
So they were very large and surprisingly enough, not more than, say, a few times faster than a human being could do the addition.

428
00:32:09,880 --> 00:32:15,140
Each computer would have to be instructed or programmed to do a specified task.

429
00:32:16,220 --> 00:32:22,420
Basically, a computer is some electronic circuits who only care about being on or off.

430
00:32:22,421 --> 00:32:29,300
and therefore you send it a list of instructions that tell a series of circuits to be on or off.

431
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:33,240
You can do numerical calculations just using one and zero.

432
00:32:33,440 --> 00:32:35,040
It's a binary arithmetic system.

433
00:32:35,680 --> 00:32:40,020
And computers basically are capable today of doing ones and zeros

434
00:32:40,021 --> 00:32:43,440
and the instructions tell it how you want those and in what pattern

435
00:32:43,441 --> 00:32:46,440
and that basically allows it to be very functional,

436
00:32:46,441 --> 00:32:51,180
have a wide variety of things that it can do based only upon those instructions that you send it.

437
00:32:52,420 --> 00:32:57,840
Hence the punch cards, with holes triggering circuits to turn on or off.

438
00:32:58,520 --> 00:33:00,920
Punch cards were in fact the first software.

439
00:33:02,680 --> 00:33:07,660
Software is basically the instruction stream that makes the computer do what you want it to do.

440
00:33:08,880 --> 00:33:13,280
It's interesting because you can make the computer do it either crudely or elegantly,

441
00:33:13,480 --> 00:33:16,760
and so software is both art and science in the way that it comes together.

442
00:33:16,761 --> 00:33:26,620
Business soon saw office applications for the calculating tabulators and began incorporating them into offices, often to handle payroll functions.

443
00:33:27,980 --> 00:33:31,740
The problem was, these early machines were the size of offices.

444
00:33:37,000 --> 00:33:45,420
Computers began replacing their thousands of vacuum tubes with transistors in the 1950s, becoming smaller and more energy efficient in the process.

445
00:33:46,420 --> 00:33:50,220
Integrated circuits and microchips shrunk the computer even smaller.

446
00:33:51,040 --> 00:33:53,060
Soon they could fit neatly on the top of a desk.

447
00:33:54,260 --> 00:33:59,380
Born in the 60s, the personal computer began reaching into offices in the 1970s,

448
00:33:59,381 --> 00:34:02,460
and suddenly offices were moving at the speed of light.

449
00:34:03,720 --> 00:34:07,460
The computer completely automated the ability to generate documents,

450
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:11,720
so by editing it would then allow you to reprint the document

451
00:34:11,721 --> 00:34:16,240
without the serious labor of having someone have to re-keystroke every single thing.

452
00:34:17,160 --> 00:34:20,700
Change fonts, shuffle paragraphs, correct mistakes.

453
00:34:21,400 --> 00:34:22,560
Goodbye, typewriter.

454
00:34:23,440 --> 00:34:27,840
Almost all of the functions of the typewriter have now been replaced by the computer.

455
00:34:28,660 --> 00:34:35,960
You can even take a computer and use a font which looks like an old typewriter and print a letter.

456
00:34:37,140 --> 00:34:39,620
The days of the old punch cards are gone,

457
00:34:39,621 --> 00:34:43,920
and software disks with complicated programs are downloaded into our computers,

458
00:34:44,720 --> 00:34:48,940
allowing us to do word processing and spreadsheets with a click of a mouse.

459
00:34:49,780 --> 00:34:52,160
Communication has been greatly speeded up.

460
00:34:52,540 --> 00:34:56,360
I think it's really wonderful to see people send a message out

461
00:34:56,361 --> 00:35:00,780
and within 30 seconds get a message back from someone across the country.

462
00:35:01,740 --> 00:35:06,300
Now, just like phones, computers can talk to each other using local networks,

463
00:35:06,301 --> 00:35:09,740
providing instant access to all kinds of electronic information.

464
00:35:10,740 --> 00:35:13,500
The computer gradually develops a digital nervous system

465
00:35:13,501 --> 00:35:16,760
in which they're all connected to each other and they can communicate all the time.

466
00:35:18,100 --> 00:35:22,400
Not just all the computers in an office, but all the computers in the world.

467
00:35:23,520 --> 00:35:24,680
Welcome to the Internet,

468
00:35:25,120 --> 00:35:29,320
which began as a way for government research organizations to share information.

469
00:35:30,980 --> 00:35:33,980
Now it's a way to trade stocks, send memos,

470
00:35:33,981 --> 00:35:36,000
buy compact discs

471
00:35:36,001 --> 00:35:38,680
or auction off a vintage vacuum tube.

472
00:35:41,020 --> 00:35:43,060
And computers keep getting better and better,

473
00:35:43,420 --> 00:35:44,740
more powerful and capable,

474
00:35:45,280 --> 00:35:46,520
faster and faster.

475
00:35:47,300 --> 00:35:50,100
It's been a mistake in the past to bet against the hardware.

476
00:35:50,640 --> 00:35:52,520
That's always been wrong,

477
00:35:52,800 --> 00:35:54,880
and therefore there is no reason to suspect

478
00:35:54,881 --> 00:35:56,840
that we should bet against the hardware in the future.

479
00:35:57,360 --> 00:35:59,620
Transistors will get faster, they will get more dense,

480
00:36:00,080 --> 00:36:01,840
and we'll be more limited by our creativity

481
00:36:01,841 --> 00:36:03,980
than by what the technology will allow us to do.

482
00:36:05,380 --> 00:36:07,820
Today's office flies along in warp drive,

483
00:36:08,260 --> 00:36:09,640
and therein lies a problem.

484
00:36:10,160 --> 00:36:11,740
Our brains can't go that fast.

485
00:36:12,400 --> 00:36:13,860
Well, if you take me, for example,

486
00:36:14,080 --> 00:36:19,160
I still tend to write at least the initial first draft of what I'm doing,

487
00:36:19,600 --> 00:36:21,140
longhand, on paper,

488
00:36:21,620 --> 00:36:23,520
and then I use a computer to edit,

489
00:36:23,521 --> 00:36:27,000
because my brain works as fast as my hand,

490
00:36:27,360 --> 00:36:30,940
and I find that this is the best way of keeping my thoughts in order

491
00:36:30,941 --> 00:36:34,980
without losing track of the main theme.

492
00:36:36,320 --> 00:36:38,380
Ignoring predictions of its demise,

493
00:36:38,960 --> 00:36:40,480
paper hasn't quite left us,

494
00:36:40,640 --> 00:36:42,980
despite the proliferation of electronic documents.

495
00:36:44,080 --> 00:36:47,300
In fact, we're producing more paper now than we ever did.

496
00:36:48,220 --> 00:36:51,160
We need paper much more in the computer age.

497
00:36:51,540 --> 00:36:52,260
Think about it.

498
00:36:52,400 --> 00:36:54,320
Look how many times you print out your email

499
00:36:54,321 --> 00:36:59,660
or you want to issue documents or pamphlets that go out

500
00:36:59,661 --> 00:37:00,400
or the books.

501
00:37:00,401 --> 00:37:03,160
there's never been more books than there are today.

502
00:37:03,760 --> 00:37:06,600
And you can't take your computer into the bathtub to read it.

503
00:37:06,640 --> 00:37:08,200
You can't take it on the bus.

504
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:10,220
The majority of people don't have laptops.

505
00:37:10,840 --> 00:37:15,200
So paper is still a very viable part of our economy and our culture.

506
00:37:16,440 --> 00:37:17,140
Great.

507
00:37:17,480 --> 00:37:21,040
Information bombards us so quickly that our offices will have to figure out

508
00:37:21,041 --> 00:37:25,340
how to staunch the flow and sort it all out just to get anything done.

509
00:37:25,341 --> 00:37:35,340
Carpal Tunnel Syndrome, an inflammation of tendons in the hand and wrist that afflicts 15% of high-risk professionals,

510
00:37:35,820 --> 00:37:37,860
is aggravated by repetitive acts.

511
00:37:38,480 --> 00:37:42,440
Like typing, Office Wonders will return on Modern Marvels.

512
00:37:45,460 --> 00:37:49,200
Not all office innovation is the product of complex technology.

513
00:37:49,900 --> 00:37:52,020
In fact, innovations in paper continue.

514
00:37:52,800 --> 00:37:57,160
And like many breakthroughs, it's hard to gauge the need until the product gets out there.

515
00:37:58,080 --> 00:37:59,800
Take 3M's post-it note.

516
00:38:00,420 --> 00:38:03,600
One of our researchers in Center Research, Dr. Spence Silver,

517
00:38:04,280 --> 00:38:10,060
made samples of a new adhesive that didn't stick very tightly.

518
00:38:10,820 --> 00:38:14,860
And when I was singing in the church choir in the early 70s,

519
00:38:15,860 --> 00:38:21,140
I decided that what I needed was a bookmark that would stick lightly,

520
00:38:21,141 --> 00:38:24,840
that wouldn't fall out between Wednesday choir practice and time to sing.

521
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:28,260
Fry decided he was on to something with his sticky bookmark.

522
00:38:28,700 --> 00:38:32,520
Here are some samples of the very first post-it notes that I made.

523
00:38:33,620 --> 00:38:39,020
And I made them in these colors and in yellow.

524
00:38:40,140 --> 00:38:45,160
And these were the first ones that we sold were in yellow.

525
00:38:46,360 --> 00:38:50,360
Of course, before they were invented, no one realized they needed post-it notes.

526
00:38:50,361 --> 00:38:58,080
You have to create a market that hasn't existed before and show the customers why they need to use those products.

527
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:01,840
The public got the message on Post-it notes pretty fast.

528
00:39:02,520 --> 00:39:06,860
3M now has over 90 different Post-it products, including computer applications.

529
00:39:07,940 --> 00:39:11,120
It's a very effective, succinct piece of communication.

530
00:39:11,860 --> 00:39:16,240
People are forced to really shrink their message down to fit the note.

531
00:39:16,241 --> 00:39:21,100
So we get rid of all the unwieldy words that aren't necessary to get your message through.

532
00:39:22,200 --> 00:39:25,740
Who would have thought that communicating less would be an office survival technique?

533
00:39:27,120 --> 00:39:30,380
Computers and the Internet are an Alaskan pipeline of information,

534
00:39:30,840 --> 00:39:35,380
flooding our cubicles, piling up on our stacks of memorandums and faxes and copies.

535
00:39:35,960 --> 00:39:38,580
Traditional offices are ill-equipped to handle it.

536
00:39:39,200 --> 00:39:43,440
One has to wonder also, well, a lot of this work may or may not be useful work.

537
00:39:43,660 --> 00:39:44,620
Is it adding to anything?

538
00:39:44,621 --> 00:39:47,900
and the person who's doing it, of course, is going to say,

539
00:39:48,040 --> 00:39:50,220
well, it's very useful, valuable work.

540
00:39:50,900 --> 00:39:54,160
No one is going to say I'm doing something that doesn't need to be done.

541
00:39:56,000 --> 00:39:59,220
Offices are finding new ways to deal with the tide of information.

542
00:40:00,740 --> 00:40:05,000
There'll be a wide variety of new devices that will come out.

543
00:40:05,001 --> 00:40:10,160
These devices will take the place of telephones, as they exist today,

544
00:40:10,220 --> 00:40:11,740
on the wall or on the desk.

545
00:40:12,240 --> 00:40:14,480
Maybe they'll be in your microwave oven.

546
00:40:14,620 --> 00:40:16,560
Maybe they'll be on your refrigerator door.

547
00:40:17,100 --> 00:40:22,320
Maybe you'll step into your automobile and, while you're driving to work,

548
00:40:22,720 --> 00:40:28,480
talk to someone through a very easy-to-use interface without having to hold something in your hand.

549
00:40:29,640 --> 00:40:35,340
Devices in the future will no longer look exactly like the standard telephone we know of today,

550
00:40:35,420 --> 00:40:38,500
nor will they look like the standard computer that we use today.

551
00:40:38,501 --> 00:40:44,640
they'll be adapted to fit the way you're used to working or the way you're used to living.

552
00:40:45,780 --> 00:40:50,780
How about telephones that automatically route your business calls to whatever phone you're nearest,

553
00:40:51,040 --> 00:40:54,840
even if you're a thousand miles from your office, just by punching in a code?

554
00:40:58,900 --> 00:41:03,040
Video conferencing, in which multiple cameras home in on whoever's talking.

555
00:41:03,041 --> 00:41:08,680
now if you could fast forward in the future wouldn't it be nice to have not just a two-dimensional

556
00:41:08,681 --> 00:41:12,760
representation of the people from the far end but more of a three-dimensional representation

557
00:41:12,761 --> 00:41:19,800
and holography in the future may be the way that we communicate not just the flat image of a person

558
00:41:19,801 --> 00:41:26,100
but a three-dimensional image of a person that is part of a video conference maybe they'll call it a

559
00:41:26,101 --> 00:41:26,940
Apollo Conference.

560
00:41:29,060 --> 00:41:32,080
Imagine having a conference with someone who isn't really there.

561
00:41:32,900 --> 00:41:35,400
How about having it in an office that doesn't exist?

562
00:41:36,240 --> 00:41:39,280
Jennifer Johnson runs a marketing firm called Johnson & Company

563
00:41:39,281 --> 00:41:42,160
with 16 employees from a virtual office.

564
00:41:42,640 --> 00:41:45,760
We have our corporate headquarters on the Internet

565
00:41:45,761 --> 00:41:48,480
as opposed to any bricks and mortar facility.

566
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:50,600
In fact, when people ask me,

567
00:41:50,601 --> 00:41:53,620
when you get bigger, are you going to want to have a physical office?

568
00:41:53,940 --> 00:41:55,600
I'd view that as a step backwards.

569
00:41:56,100 --> 00:42:01,520
What we do is give people the opportunity and ultimate flexibility to work out of their own homes,

570
00:42:01,860 --> 00:42:06,380
to determine their hours, how they want to get their work done, when they want to get it done.

571
00:42:07,320 --> 00:42:11,700
Johnson's co-workers connect and communicate, strategizing for clients,

572
00:42:12,380 --> 00:42:16,560
analyzing data, and doing market research across four time zones,

573
00:42:16,561 --> 00:42:18,840
through the phone and the Internet.

574
00:42:20,740 --> 00:42:23,220
Johnson can review a memo in California at 11,

575
00:42:23,221 --> 00:42:31,620
and instantly send it to co-worker Jordan Karbowitz at her apartment in New Jersey at 2.

576
00:42:34,560 --> 00:42:38,640
We do have our sights set, though, on going international.

577
00:42:38,840 --> 00:42:41,400
I've had dialogues with companies in Europe and even Asia

578
00:42:41,401 --> 00:42:48,640
because to me it would be so exciting for a 100% virtual company to be able to offer global services.

579
00:42:49,720 --> 00:42:52,620
But what about those people still working in the brick-and-mortar buildings?

580
00:42:53,220 --> 00:43:01,260
There will always be some sort of office, some sort of central place where people can go,

581
00:43:01,261 --> 00:43:04,340
because after all, human beings are social animals.

582
00:43:05,000 --> 00:43:08,120
We do learn from each other. We interact with each other.

583
00:43:08,600 --> 00:43:12,640
The office is a place of work as well as a place of social interaction.

584
00:43:12,820 --> 00:43:15,460
So yes, I think that there will always be an office.

585
00:43:16,460 --> 00:43:19,820
Those brick-and-mortar guys are finding ways to innovate, too.

586
00:43:20,480 --> 00:43:23,640
Take John Ferrara, who runs a software company called Goldmine.

587
00:43:24,400 --> 00:43:28,220
Goldmine makes a software application that works like an electronic scheduler,

588
00:43:28,700 --> 00:43:34,480
coordinating and interconnecting the workday priorities and plans of everybody in an office, plus clients.

589
00:43:34,481 --> 00:43:39,880
It ties people together so that they can all keep track of the relationships,

590
00:43:40,340 --> 00:43:43,340
what's pending, who's going to do it, and what's been done.

591
00:43:43,341 --> 00:43:49,880
Not surprisingly, this cutting-edge software company takes a new approach to the traditional office dynamic.

592
00:43:50,960 --> 00:43:55,080
They want their employees to be more than just parts in the machine that is the office.

593
00:43:56,060 --> 00:43:58,100
They want them to be happy.

594
00:43:59,560 --> 00:44:02,560
Goldmine thinks happy workers are more productive ones.

595
00:44:04,200 --> 00:44:06,460
This is not your father's office.

596
00:44:06,720 --> 00:44:12,840
This is a whole new way to get the message out, to communicate, and it always goes back to communication.

597
00:44:13,340 --> 00:44:15,300
That's what an office is all about.

598
00:44:16,020 --> 00:44:18,640
And that's what all this technology is supposed to do.

599
00:44:19,660 --> 00:44:21,460
But it can be overwhelming.

600
00:44:22,260 --> 00:44:24,060
Sometimes you need to step back from it all.

601
00:44:24,980 --> 00:44:26,880
That's why we have the office here at the beach.

602
00:44:27,400 --> 00:44:33,440
That's why we have benefits enable people to go to the gym and work out in the morning or at lunch

603
00:44:33,441 --> 00:44:38,440
or go to the beach and run on the beach or surf in the morning or in the afternoon after work.

604
00:44:38,540 --> 00:44:42,720
I work from 7 to 4 just so I can get off at 4 and surf until dark.

605
00:44:43,340 --> 00:44:47,540
High-tech software company with an office on the curl of a wave.

606
00:44:48,040 --> 00:44:51,060
Surfing the net one minute, surfing the Pacific the next.

607
00:44:51,760 --> 00:44:57,780
All these high-tech marvels just might help us busy bees get a bit more low-tech with our hectic lives.

608
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:03,360
The office of the future is a pretty exciting place when you think about it.

609
00:45:07,500 --> 00:45:09,800
Almost makes you look forward to going in tomorrow.

610
00:45:11,180 --> 00:45:11,880
Almost.

611
00:45:13,340 --> 00:45:18,540
Former U.S. Marine, drill instructor, Vietnam veteran.

612
00:45:18,960 --> 00:45:22,100
Now he's facing his greatest challenge, your questions.

613
00:45:22,240 --> 00:45:24,040
What does GI stand for?

614
00:45:24,200 --> 00:45:27,320
What kind of grab-astic, unorganized question is that?

615
00:45:27,360 --> 00:45:29,220
Um, join R. Lee Ermey.

616
00:45:29,420 --> 00:45:30,300
Can it, numbnuts!

617
00:45:30,340 --> 00:45:31,360
Now listen up.

618
00:45:31,620 --> 00:45:33,000
Want to know how to drive a tank?

619
00:45:33,040 --> 00:45:34,380
How a gas mask works?

620
00:45:34,400 --> 00:45:36,000
What dog tags are used for?

621
00:45:36,060 --> 00:45:38,980
I know all the answers, so ask the questions, maggot!

622
00:45:38,981 --> 00:45:43,000
Mail call, Sunday nights at 10 on the History Channel.

623
00:45:43,001 --> 00:45:44,720
Ask at your own risk.

624
00:45:45,160 --> 00:45:48,240
Now an exclusive offer from the History Channel.

